course: dictionary
lesson: 5012
title: The infinitive, the basic form of a verb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Every verb has a basic form that dictionaries use to list it. This is the infinitive. It often has «to» in front: to run, to be, to eat. Verbs change their shape in sentences (ran, is, eating), but the infinitive stays the same.
explain: The bare infinitive has no «to»: run, be, eat. You will see it after words like can, will and must: «I can swim», «You must eat». Here the bare infinitive is needed, so «I can to swim» is wrong.
explain: Some grammar rules are younger than you would think. The «never split an infinitive» rule says no word should come between «to» and the verb. It was copied from Latin in the 1800s, so it is not an old law of English. Most modern guides say «to boldly go» is fine.
fact: The infinitive is the basic form of a verb, often with «to» in front: to run, to be, to eat.
fact: The bare infinitive has no «to»: run, be, eat.
fact: Dictionaries list verbs by their infinitive, so look up «ran» under «run».
fact: A split infinitive has a word between «to» and the verb, as in «to boldly go».
fact: The rule against split infinitives came from Latin in the 1800s, and most modern guides say it is fine to split them.
exercise: Which is the basic form of the verb «ate»? | *eat | ate | eating | eaten
exercise: Which is the bare infinitive, with no «to»? | to be | *be | was | being
exercise: Which phrase has a split infinitive? | boldly go | to go | go boldly | *to boldly go
test: Which is the bare infinitive of «wrote»? | wrote | to write | writing | *write
test: In «I must finish my work», which word is the bare infinitive? | my | *finish | work | must
exam: Dictionaries list a verb by which form? | the plural | the adjective | *the infinitive | the past tense
